  "name": "Example: Solving a Polynomial Inequality Using a Given Graph",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/55018a35718d655dc2b0d67692768e7c78ee9c064f137b31435a204280734c08.png)\n\nGiven the sketch of the polynomial $y = p(x)$ shown above, find the solution to $p(x) < 0$.",
  "worked_solution": "To solve the inequality $p(x) <    0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=p(x)$ lies below the $x$-axis. These intervals are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/141519a0d0abc0d1545d940aba1fd52b15e96d81b0c10560960df2710da4937f.png)\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is\n\n$$\nx \\in (-\\infty, -3) \\cup (-3, -1) \\cup (0,2).\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** Notice that this is a strict inequality, so we do not include the roots in our solution.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
